Subject: [PATCH] audio: use /dev/audio on substrate (Sun/SADA device path)

Substrate's audio device is the Sun/NetBSD-style /dev/audio (alias to
/dev/audio0), not the OSS /dev/dsp.  SDL's NetBSD audio backend is the
one compiled in for substrate (the build routes the linux host case to
it), but SDL_audiodev.c only maps _PATH_DEV_DSP to "/dev/audio" when
__NETBSD__ / __OPENBSD__ is defined.  The substrate cross compiler
defines __substrate__, not __NETBSD__, so _PATH_DEV_DSP fell back to
"/dev/dsp" — which does not exist on substrate.

SDL_EnumUnixAudioDevices() therefore found zero output devices, and
NETBSDAUDIO_OpenDevice() (called with devname == NULL by
SDL_OpenAudioDevice(NULL, ...)) resolved the default device name via
SDL_GetAudioDeviceName(0), got NULL, and failed with
"No such audio device".  Every SDL application (PsyMP3) then opened no
audio device, no audio thread/callback ran, and the playback clock —
which PsyMP3 advances from the SDL callback — stayed stuck at 0:00.

Add __substrate__ to the condition so the default device path is
/dev/audio, matching the NetBSD/OpenBSD branch.

--- a/src/audio/SDL_audiodev.c
+++ b/src/audio/SDL_audiodev.c
@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@
 #include "SDL_audiodev_c.h"

 #ifndef _PATH_DEV_DSP
-#if defined(__NETBSD__) || defined(__OPENBSD__)
+#if defined(__NETBSD__) || defined(__OPENBSD__) || defined(__substrate__)
 #define _PATH_DEV_DSP "/dev/audio"
 #else
 #define _PATH_DEV_DSP "/dev/dsp"
